tl;dr for those in need:
*Is it fun? Initially. But gradually fun goes away.
*Is it worth the money? I got my share of fun from it, but I enjoyed the time of release, when everyone was learning the game. New buyers will most likely get thrown into existing meta and will be forced to do what works instead of experimenting, thus highly crippling the fun curve.
*Is this a cashgrab? I'd have to say no, since devs are doing something (not really sure what tho, but hey, they released a big patch recently), this game earned enough to warrant some maintenance after release (even if the success faded quickly as seen in player numbers flop) and ubisoft did revitalise rainbow six.
*Would I buy it if I knew what I know now? No. Its a mix of good emotions turning into continuous frustration and disappointment. There are other games which bring more entertainment value and there also are games with similar theme and mechanics in the make, I'd rather wait for them.
For those who'd like a long rant, let me share my experience with this game.
When I first bought and played it it was amazing. Good graphics, interesting mechanics, 12 distinct classes... just wow. Hours just flew by playing the game: trying all the classes, enjoying game modes, kicking as* getting my own as* kicked... Spent 60 hours w/out even noticing.
But then I had an urge to get better in this game and went on to look for information on how to improve. Now, what I found was pretty disheartening (based on pre-lawbringer patch experience, havent really played the game much after):
*You have to play one of the top tier classes to expect a fair chance (4-5 out of 12, really?);
*You have to only use safe attacks for optimal gameplay in duels/brawl, cause otherwise you're hurting urself (1-3 techs per class, out of whole lot);
*Some classes were miles ahead of others in certain modes (warlord, peacekeepr in duels + brawls);
*You have to have max gear score to have a fighting chance against other max gear score chars in dominion/elim/skirmish modes (the difference between having the gear and not having it was HUGE, giving geared players insanse killing sprees against people who didn't have gear).
Fair enough, I picked a top tier class (conqueror) and went to train. Sure, it went well and winrate soared, went to 80%+ winrate in both dominion and duel, 70%+ skirmish/elim. As I was approaching 120+ hours played I suddenly started asking myself questions: "Whether this hame was really fun?" and "Whether it had any future?"
Answer at that time was no. And here's why:
*Matchmaking was garbage, you can play very weak players for few matches then instatnly very strong players, completely putting you off your game due to lack of consistency (you steamroll a newbie, slack a bit, get a proplayer and then struggle to focus and therefore lose);
*matchmaking has no sense of accomplishment. League of Legends has tiers you can climb when you win, in For Honor however you don't. You have 80 percent + winrate and you still farm significantly weaker players until you lose your 2 statistical matches out of 10;
*Losing matches. Sometimes you get outclassed. Sometimes you lose to other factors like lag, like the other player using bugs or overpowered techs. (Headbutt spam anyone?);
*Devs did almost nothing to change that while the game was still hot on the market, finally taking action only after community expressed its sheer disappointment (search For Honor blackout), but way too late at this point to gather some of its playerbase back (check steamcharts for For Honor playerbase).
At that point I got fed up with the situation and simply stopped playing. Other worthy contributors to stopping:
*p2p servers. Just no. Lag if the host is laggy? Game stopping whenever someone leaves/joins? Games crashing? Just no.
*Lack of competitive gameplay. The only competition was fan/sponsor organised outside the game. Frankly, thats too much of a hassle to register (not to mention the late hours it begins at and lasts to). Why can't this be as easy as League of Legends, Counter-Strike or Dota. Grab a quick ranked game or two and get ur sense of accomplishment satisfied. Hell, the tourneys are not even worth as you play the same classes doing the same thing again and again (once again, only few classes being viable and them having 1-3 viable techs is just... boring).
Just came back to try out "lawbringer" patch. Played it for 3 hours and came up to conclusion that it does not warrant a comeback.
*Same people in queues (lack of playerbase);
*Still plenty of overpowered techs;
*Quality of game seems worse, more sluggish;
*Even more crashes.
Therefore, quitting again until next big patch. Coming back to test it though, since I still have hope in this game and I payed the money for it anyway.

This game, being supposedly horror, is weak, but as a SURVIVOR horror game it stands just a tad out;
what do I mean? it's simple, it didn't scare me - if we don't count some random jumpscares every now and again - and it was not a graphical problems, for me the game looks great and has some nice scenarios, but sometimes this game just turns into a shooter, exemple in the "Dungeon of Pain" when the spiders came, I don't have a big fear of spiders so it felt more like a shooter -or a bit like minecraft with a rifle *chuckles*- and the fault of checkpoints adds to the "you have to survive" feel more than to the "now you are ♥♥♥♥ed up boy" horror games usually give;
Horror games with guns never work out that scary for me -yes, I've playied Cry of Fear- and after those games I can simply go into a dark room no problem, where as a horror game without, we can just admire how weak and fragile our bodies are, weapons take that feel away, since no one really wants to go close when killing a demon with a rifle -which of course ain't Doom guy-
So yeah, not to spoopy, but hella fun -would be more if my computer could run the enemies properly tho-
About graphics, they are ok; music is awesome but comes at awkward moments; story is "who gives a ♥♥♥♥ I wanna shoot♥♥♥♥♥♥quot; and spoopyness factor is "that friend which screams 'boo' everytime"

